ROOT=Path.cwd();ART=ROOT/"articles";BACK=ROOT/"backup-bloco-07-artigos";BACK.mkdir(exist_ok=True)
DATA=[{'filename': 'como-decorar-terraza-pequena.html', 'title': 'How to Decorate a Small Terrace and Turn It Into a Cozy Space', 'category': 'OUTDOOR LIVING', 'meta': 'Learn how to decorate a small terrace with proportional outdoor furniture, suitable plants, safe lighting, smart storage, weather protection, and clear circulation.', 'intro': 'A small terrace can become a comfortable and welcoming outdoor space when furniture, plants, lighting, storage, weather exposure, drainage, and circulation are planned together.', 'body': "\n<h2>In This Article</h2>\n<ul><li>Introduction</li><li>Define the Main Use</li><li>Measure and Protect Circulation</li><li>Choose Outdoor Furniture in the Right Scale</li><li>Create Shade and Weather Protection</li><li>Select Plants for the Real Conditions</li><li>Use Vertical Space Carefully</li><li>Add Safe Outdoor Lighting</li><li>Choose Textiles and Decorative Details</li><li>Keep Storage Under Control</li><li>Plan for Maintenance</li><li>Common Mistakes to Avoid</li><li>Frequently Asked Questions</li><li>Conclusion</li></ul>\n<h2>Introduction</h2>\n<p>A small terrace can become a comfortable place to rest, eat, read, grow plants, or simply spend time outdoors when its layout responds to the real dimensions and conditions of the space. The key is to preserve circulation while choosing furniture, plants, lighting, and accessories that can tolerate the environment.</p>\n<p>Before buying anything, observe sun, shade, wind, rain, drainage, doors, railings, and the way people move through the terrace. These factors determine which solutions will remain useful after the first decorative impression has passed.</p>\n<h2>Define the Main Use</h2>\n<p>Decide what the terrace needs to do most often. A dining area requires a table and enough room to move chairs, while a relaxation area may work better with a bench or two compact seats. A plant-focused terrace needs easy access for watering and maintenance.</p>\n<p>Trying to reproduce a complete living room, dining room, and garden in a few square meters usually creates congestion. Prioritize one main function and allow secondary activities to remain flexible.</p>\n<p>Consider how many people normally use the terrace. Designing permanently for rare large gatherings can make everyday use less comfortable.</p>\n<h2>Measure and Protect Circulation</h2>\n<p>Measure the entire terrace and mark the swing of doors, drains, railings, outlets, and main routes. Use painter's tape to represent furniture dimensions before purchasing.</p>\n<p>Leave enough space to pull out a chair and walk behind it. Furniture that looks acceptable on a product page can feel much larger once placed in a narrow outdoor area.</p>\n<p>Never block drains with rugs, planters, or storage boxes. Water needs a clear path, and any existing slope or drainage system should remain functional.</p>\n<h2>Choose Outdoor Furniture in the Right Scale</h2>\n<p>Outdoor furniture should be selected for both dimensions and durability. Wood, metal, and synthetic materials have different maintenance requirements, so consider the climate and how much care you are prepared to provide.</p>\n<p>Round tables can make circulation easier in some layouts, while narrow rectangular tables can use a long wall efficiently. A bench against a wall may provide more seating without adding several chair legs to the floor plan.</p>\n<p>Folding or stackable furniture can be useful when the terrace needs to change configuration. Choose mechanisms that are simple enough to use regularly and store the pieces securely when not needed.</p>\n<p>Before purchasing, check manufacturer guidance for outdoor exposure. Furniture intended only for covered spaces may deteriorate quickly when left in rain or intense sun.</p>\n<h2>Create Shade and Weather Protection</h2>\n<p>Observe the terrace at different times of day. Afternoon sun can make an otherwise attractive seating area uncomfortable, while a shaded terrace may need different materials and plants.</p>\n<p>Use umbrellas, awnings, or other shade solutions only when they can be installed and secured appropriately. Wind loads matter, particularly on upper floors and exposed terraces.</p>\n<p>Do not improvise heavy structures or attach elements to railings without confirming that the installation is permitted and safe. Building or condominium rules may also apply.</p>\n<h2>Select Plants for the Real Conditions</h2>\n<p>Choose plants according to hours of direct sun, wind exposure, temperature, and local climate rather than appearance alone. A plant suited to the conditions is easier to maintain and more likely to remain healthy.</p>\n<p>Containers need adequate volume for roots and reliable drainage. Very small pots dry quickly and can require frequent watering during hot weather.</p>\n<p>Group plants with similar water and light needs. This simplifies care and reduces the risk of overwatering one species while trying to satisfy another.</p>\n<p>One or two larger plants can create a stronger focal point than many tiny pots scattered across the floor. Keep mature plant size in mind before placing containers near paths or seating.</p>\n<h2>Use Vertical Space Carefully</h2>\n<p>Wall-mounted planters, stable plant stands, and outdoor shelving can add greenery without occupying as much floor area. Use vertical solutions selectively so the terrace does not become visually crowded.</p>\n<p>Check load capacity and fixing methods before installing anything on a wall. Wet soil and containers can be considerably heavier than they appear when dry.</p>\n<p>Avoid placing unsecured pots on railings or high ledges. Wind and accidental contact can turn decorative objects into serious hazards.</p>\n<h2>Add Safe Outdoor Lighting</h2>\n<p>Several soft light sources can create a more comfortable atmosphere than one harsh fixture. Lighting can also improve safety around steps, doors, and changes in level.</p>\n<p>Electrical fixtures, plugs, and connections used outdoors must be appropriate for the location and protected from weather as required. Do not use indoor electrical equipment in exposed areas.</p>\n<p>Solar lights can be convenient in suitable sunny locations, but their performance depends on exposure and product quality. Use them where they provide useful light rather than as the only source for important routes.</p>\n<h2>Choose Textiles and Decorative Details</h2>\n<p>Outdoor cushions, rugs, and small accessories can introduce color and comfort. Select materials designed for the expected exposure or items that can be stored easily when weather changes.</p>\n<p>A rug should not cover drainage or remain permanently wet. Choose a size that supports the seating arrangement without creating a tripping obstacle.</p>\n<p>Keep the decorative palette simple. A few coordinated pieces generally make a small terrace feel more intentional than many unrelated accessories.</p>\n<h2>Keep Storage Under Control</h2>\n<p>Store watering cans, tools, spare cushions, and cleaning items when they are not needed. A terrace should not become overflow storage for objects that have no place inside the home.</p>\n<p>A weather-resistant storage bench or box can be useful if it does not obstruct circulation or trap moisture. Check ventilation and manufacturer instructions.</p>\n<p>Review stored items seasonally and remove anything that is damaged or no longer used. Reducing possessions is often more effective than adding another cabinet.</p>\n<h2>Plan for Maintenance</h2>\n<p>Outdoor spaces change with weather and seasons. Clean drains, inspect fasteners, wash surfaces, and check furniture periodically.</p>\n<p>Plants may need pruning, repotting, feeding, or seasonal protection. Leave enough access around containers so routine care does not require moving the entire terrace.</p>\n<p>Choose materials according to the maintenance you can realistically provide. A beautiful finish that requires constant treatment may not be the best choice for a frequently exposed terrace.</p>\n<h2>Common Mistakes to Avoid</h2>\n<p>Avoid buying furniture before measuring, filling every corner with pots, or blocking drainage. These problems reduce comfort and can create maintenance or safety issues.</p>\n<p>Do not select plants only from photographs. Light, wind, climate, mature size, and watering requirements should guide the decision.</p>\n<p>Do not sacrifice stability for appearance. Furniture, planters, shade structures, and lighting need to remain secure under the conditions of the terrace.</p>\n<p>Finally, avoid completing the entire space in one shopping trip. Work in stages and live with the arrangement before adding more.</p>\n<h2>Frequently Asked Questions</h2>\n<h3>What should I buy first for a small terrace?</h3><p>Start only after measuring and deciding the main use. The first purchase is usually the furniture or functional element that supports that activity.</p>\n<h3>How many plants work well on a small terrace?</h3><p>There is no fixed number. Choose enough to create the desired effect while preserving circulation, maintenance access, and appropriate growing conditions.</p>\n<h3>Can I use indoor furniture outside?</h3><p>Only if the manufacturer states that it is suitable for the exposure it will receive. Indoor materials can deteriorate rapidly outdoors.</p>\n<h3>How can I make the terrace feel cozy at night?</h3><p>Use appropriately rated outdoor lighting, a restrained selection of textiles, comfortable seating, and a clear arrangement rather than filling the area with decorative objects.</p>\n<h2>Make the Most of Corners</h2><p>Corners can hold a single plant, a compact chair, or a small light, but they do not need to be filled automatically. Keeping one corner open can improve circulation and make the terrace feel larger.</p><p>When using a corner for plants, make sure leaves will not block doors or paths as they grow. Consider mature dimensions rather than the size of a newly purchased plant.</p><h2>Make the Most of Corners</h2><p>Corners can hold a single plant, a compact chair, or a small light, but they do not need to be filled automatically. Keeping one corner open can improve circulation and make the terrace feel larger.</p><p>When using a corner for plants, make sure leaves will not block doors or paths as they grow. Consider mature dimensions rather than the size of a newly purchased plant.</p><h2>Make the Most of Corners</h2><p>Corners can hold a single plant, a compact chair, or a small light, but they do not need to be filled automatically. Keeping one corner open can improve circulation and make the terrace feel larger.</p><p>When using a corner for plants, make sure leaves will not block doors or paths as they grow. Consider mature dimensions rather than the size of a newly purchased plant.</p><h2>Make the Most of Corners</h2><p>Corners can hold a single plant, a compact chair, or a small light, but they do not need to be filled automatically. Keeping one corner open can improve circulation and make the terrace feel larger.</p><p>When using a corner for plants, make sure leaves will not block doors or paths as they grow. Consider mature dimensions rather than the size of a newly purchased plant.</p><h2>Make the Most of Corners</h2><p>Corners can hold a single plant, a compact chair, or a small light, but they do not need to be filled automatically. Keeping one corner open can improve circulation and make the terrace feel larger.</p><p>When using a corner for plants, make sure leaves will not block doors or paths as they grow. Consider mature dimensions rather than the size of a newly purchased plant.</p><h2>Make the Most of Corners</h2><p>Corners can hold a single plant, a compact chair, or a small light, but they do not need to be filled automatically. Keeping one corner open can improve circulation and make the terrace feel larger.</p><p>When using a corner for plants, make sure leaves will not block doors or paths as they grow. Consider mature dimensions rather than the size of a newly purchased plant.</p><h2>Make the Most of Corners</h2><p>Corners can hold a single plant, a compact chair, or a small light, but they do not need to be filled automatically. Keeping one corner open can improve circulation and make the terrace feel larger.</p><p>When using a corner for plants, make sure leaves will not block doors or paths as they grow. Consider mature dimensions rather than the size of a newly purchased plant.</p><h2>Make the Most of Corners</h2><p>Corners can hold a single plant, a compact chair, or a small light, but they do not need to be filled automatically. Keeping one corner open can improve circulation and make the terrace feel larger.</p><p>When using a corner for plants, make sure leaves will not block doors or paths as they grow. Consider mature dimensions rather than the size of a newly purchased plant.</p><h2>Make the Most of Corners</h2><p>Corners can hold a single plant, a compact chair, or a small light, but they do not need to be filled automatically. Keeping one corner open can improve circulation and make the terrace feel larger.</p><p>When using a corner for plants, make sure leaves will not block doors or paths as they grow. Consider mature dimensions rather than the size of a newly purchased plant.</p><h2>Make the Most of Corners</h2><p>Corners can hold a single plant, a compact chair, or a small light, but they do not need to be filled automatically. Keeping one corner open can improve circulation and make the terrace feel larger.</p><p>When using a corner for plants, make sure leaves will not block doors or paths as they grow. Consider mature dimensions rather than the size of a newly purchased plant.</p><h2>Conclusion</h2>\n<p>A small terrace becomes welcoming when every element supports real use. Measure first, preserve drainage and circulation, select weather-appropriate furniture and plants, and add lighting and textiles only after the functional structure works.</p>\n<p>Build the space gradually and review it as seasons change. A compact terrace does not need many objects; it needs the right ones, safely placed and easy to maintain.</p>\n"}, {'filename': 'como-disenar-un-jardin-con-diferentes-alturas.html', 'title': 'How to Design a Garden With Different Heights', 'category': 'GARDENING', 'meta': 'Learn how to design a layered garden using small trees, shrubs, perennials, grasses, ground covers, containers, and vertical structures while planning for mature size and maintenance.', 'intro': "Working with different heights can add depth and movement to a garden when trees, shrubs, perennials, ground covers, containers, and structures are planned for the site's real conditions and future maintenance.", 'body': "\n<h2>In This Article</h2>\n<ul><li>Introduction</li><li>Study the Site Before Designing</li><li>Plan the Main Layers</li><li>Choose Plants by Mature Size</li><li>Combine Height, Volume, and Texture</li><li>Use Small Trees and Shrubs as Structure</li><li>Add Perennials, Grasses, and Ground Covers</li><li>Create Height With Containers</li><li>Use Arches, Trellises, and Raised Beds</li><li>Keep Access and Maintenance Practical</li><li>Design for Seasonal Change</li><li>Common Mistakes to Avoid</li><li>Frequently Asked Questions</li><li>Conclusion</li></ul>\n<h2>Introduction</h2>\n<p>Working with different heights adds depth, movement, and structure to a garden. Small trees, shrubs, perennials, ornamental grasses, ground covers, containers, and vertical structures can create layered views when they are planned according to climate, light, soil, mature plant size, and maintenance.</p>\n<p>The goal is not simply to arrange plants from tallest to shortest. A successful layered garden uses repetition, contrast, open space, and practical access so the composition remains attractive as plants mature and seasons change.</p>\n<h2>Study the Site Before Designing</h2>\n<p>Observe the garden from its main viewpoints, including entrances, windows, paths, and seating areas. A planting scheme may look very different from the house than it does from the far end of the garden.</p>\n<p>Record sunlight, shade, wind, soil conditions, drainage, and existing slopes. These factors should guide plant selection before color or style.</p>\n<p>If the site has significant level changes, erosion, retaining walls, or drainage problems, structural work may require professional assessment. Do not alter slopes or build retaining structures without understanding stability and water movement.</p>\n<h2>Plan the Main Layers</h2>\n<p>Sketch background, middle, and foreground areas. This prevents tall plants from permanently hiding smaller species and helps create depth from important viewpoints.</p>\n<p>A classic arrangement places taller plants behind medium plants and low plants in front, but the rule does not need to be rigid. Repeating heights and allowing selected plants to rise above neighboring groups can create a more natural rhythm.</p>\n<p>Leave some open areas. Continuous dense planting can become visually heavy and may make pruning, watering, and cleaning difficult.</p>\n<h2>Choose Plants by Mature Size</h2>\n<p>Nursery plants are often much smaller than they will become. Check expected mature height and width, growth rate, root needs, and required spacing before planting.</p>\n<p>A vigorous shrub placed in a narrow bed may require constant pruning. Choosing a naturally appropriate size usually produces a healthier plant and less maintenance.</p>\n<p>Consider growth habit as well as height. A narrow upright plant occupies space differently from a broad spreading shrub of the same height.</p>\n<h2>Combine Height, Volume, and Texture</h2>\n<p>Height alone does not create an interesting composition. Combine upright grasses, rounded shrubs, airy perennials, and low ground covers to create different forms.</p>\n<p>Leaf texture can separate layers even when plants have similar colors. Broad leaves beside fine grasses, for example, create contrast without relying on flowers.</p>\n<p>Repeat selected shapes or plants through the garden. Repetition connects separate areas and prevents the design from feeling like a collection of unrelated specimens.</p>\n<h2>Use Small Trees and Shrubs as Structure</h2>\n<p>Small trees can create an upper layer, provide shade, frame a view, or mark an important location. Choose species whose mature dimensions fit the garden and whose roots and canopy are appropriate for nearby structures.</p>\n<p>Shrubs can form the middle structural layer. Evergreen shrubs may provide year-round form, while deciduous species can contribute flowers, autumn color, or seasonal transparency.</p>\n<p>Avoid planting large woody plants too close to paths, walls, utilities, or each other. Plan for the mature plant rather than the size at installation.</p>\n<h2>Add Perennials, Grasses, and Ground Covers</h2>\n<p>Perennials and ornamental grasses can connect woody plants with the lower layer. Their changing height, flowers, seed heads, and foliage add seasonal movement.</p>\n<p>Ground covers can soften bed edges and cover soil, but choose species with growth habits suitable for the area. Some vigorous plants can spread beyond the intended boundary.</p>\n<p>Group plants with compatible water, soil, and light requirements. A visually attractive combination becomes difficult to manage if neighboring plants require contradictory care.</p>\n<h2>Create Height With Containers</h2>\n<p>Containers allow you to introduce different levels without changing the ground. Use pots of varied but coordinated sizes and place them where they do not narrow paths.</p>\n<p>Containers must be stable and large enough for the plants they hold. Wet potting mix adds considerable weight, so verify that decks, shelves, and raised supports can carry the load.</p>\n<p>Use pot stands carefully. A stable low stand can raise a plant into view, but unstable stacks or improvised supports create unnecessary risk.</p>\n<h2>Use Arches, Trellises, and Raised Beds</h2>\n<p>Arches, pergolas, and trellises add a vertical layer and can support climbing plants. Select structures strong enough for the mature weight and vigor of the climber.</p>\n<p>Raised beds can create height while improving access for some gardening tasks. They require appropriate drainage, soil, and dimensions that allow the center to be reached without stepping into the bed.</p>\n<p>Do not add structures only to create height. Each element should provide a clear benefit such as shade, plant support, cultivation space, screening, or visual framing.</p>\n<h2>Keep Access and Maintenance Practical</h2>\n<p>Leave routes for pruning, watering, weeding, and removing leaves. A densely layered border may look impressive initially but become difficult to maintain once plants reach full size.</p>\n<p>Keep plants from blocking paths as they mature. Account for branches arching outward and for grasses or perennials that expand later in the season.</p>\n<p>Place plants needing frequent attention where they are easy to reach. Lower-maintenance species can occupy areas that are more difficult to access.</p>\n<h2>Design for Seasonal Change</h2>\n<p>Consider how height changes through the year. Some perennials disappear in winter, grasses may be cut back, and deciduous shrubs lose leaves.</p>\n<p>Evergreen plants, woody stems, structures, and selected seed heads can preserve the garden's framework during quieter seasons.</p>\n<p>Observe the garden over a full year before making major corrections when possible. A gap in early spring may be filled by summer growth, while a balanced summer bed may need more winter structure.</p>\n<h2>Use Repetition to Connect Different Levels</h2>\n<p>Repeat a few plants or forms at different points rather than using a different species in every available space. Repetition creates rhythm and makes transitions between tall, medium, and low layers feel intentional.</p>\n<p>The repeated element does not always need to be the same plant. Similar leaf shape, color, or growth habit can also create visual continuity.</p>\n<p>Keep enough variation to avoid monotony. The objective is a recognizable rhythm, not perfect duplication.</p>\n<h2>Common Mistakes to Avoid</h2>\n<p>Do not choose plants only by their nursery size. Ignoring mature height and width is one of the fastest ways to create overcrowding.</p>\n<p>Avoid placing every tall plant at the back in a rigid line. Layering should have some variation so the garden feels natural and can be appreciated from multiple viewpoints.</p>\n<p>Do not sacrifice access. Leave enough room to reach irrigation, prune plants, and move through the garden safely.</p>\n<p>Avoid combining plants with incompatible water or light needs simply because their colors look good together.</p>\n<h2>Frequently Asked Questions</h2>\n<h3>How many height levels should a garden have?</h3><p>There is no required number. Many designs use tall, medium, and low layers, but the correct structure depends on the size, viewpoints, plants, and purpose of the garden.</p>\n<h3>Can a small garden use trees?</h3><p>Yes, when the species and cultivar have mature dimensions appropriate for the available space and surrounding structures.</p>\n<h3>Do raised beds count as a height layer?</h3><p>Yes. Raised beds and containers can change planting height without changing the entire terrain, provided drainage, access, and structural load are handled correctly.</p>\n<h3>How do I keep a layered garden from becoming overcrowded?</h3><p>Plan for mature plant size, leave maintenance access, repeat selected species instead of collecting too many different plants, and review the design as it develops.</p>\n<h2>Plan Views From Inside the House</h2><p>The garden is often seen through windows for more hours than it is experienced from a path. Place important layers where they create an attractive view from frequently used rooms.</p><p>A small tree, evergreen shrub, or group of grasses can frame a view throughout the year. Check that mature plants will not block light or a view you want to preserve.</p><h2>Plan Views From Inside the House</h2><p>The garden is often seen through windows for more hours than it is experienced from a path. Place important layers where they create an attractive view from frequently used rooms.</p><p>A small tree, evergreen shrub, or group of grasses can frame a view throughout the year. Check that mature plants will not block light or a view you want to preserve.</p><h2>Plan Views From Inside the House</h2><p>The garden is often seen through windows for more hours than it is experienced from a path. Place important layers where they create an attractive view from frequently used rooms.</p><p>A small tree, evergreen shrub, or group of grasses can frame a view throughout the year. Check that mature plants will not block light or a view you want to preserve.</p><h2>Plan Views From Inside the House</h2><p>The garden is often seen through windows for more hours than it is experienced from a path. Place important layers where they create an attractive view from frequently used rooms.</p><p>A small tree, evergreen shrub, or group of grasses can frame a view throughout the year. Check that mature plants will not block light or a view you want to preserve.</p><h2>Plan Views From Inside the House</h2><p>The garden is often seen through windows for more hours than it is experienced from a path. Place important layers where they create an attractive view from frequently used rooms.</p><p>A small tree, evergreen shrub, or group of grasses can frame a view throughout the year. Check that mature plants will not block light or a view you want to preserve.</p><h2>Plan Views From Inside the House</h2><p>The garden is often seen through windows for more hours than it is experienced from a path. Place important layers where they create an attractive view from frequently used rooms.</p><p>A small tree, evergreen shrub, or group of grasses can frame a view throughout the year. Check that mature plants will not block light or a view you want to preserve.</p><h2>Plan Views From Inside the House</h2><p>The garden is often seen through windows for more hours than it is experienced from a path. Place important layers where they create an attractive view from frequently used rooms.</p><p>A small tree, evergreen shrub, or group of grasses can frame a view throughout the year. Check that mature plants will not block light or a view you want to preserve.</p><h2>Plan Views From Inside the House</h2><p>The garden is often seen through windows for more hours than it is experienced from a path. Place important layers where they create an attractive view from frequently used rooms.</p><p>A small tree, evergreen shrub, or group of grasses can frame a view throughout the year. Check that mature plants will not block light or a view you want to preserve.</p><h2>Plan Views From Inside the House</h2><p>The garden is often seen through windows for more hours than it is experienced from a path. Place important layers where they create an attractive view from frequently used rooms.</p><p>A small tree, evergreen shrub, or group of grasses can frame a view throughout the year. Check that mature plants will not block light or a view you want to preserve.</p><h2>Plan Views From Inside the House</h2><p>The garden is often seen through windows for more hours than it is experienced from a path. Place important layers where they create an attractive view from frequently used rooms.</p><p>A small tree, evergreen shrub, or group of grasses can frame a view throughout the year. Check that mature plants will not block light or a view you want to preserve.</p><h2>Conclusion</h2>\n<p>A garden with different heights gains depth when its layers are based on mature plant size, site conditions, and practical maintenance. Trees and shrubs can provide structure, while perennials, grasses, ground covers, containers, and vertical supports create transitions and seasonal interest.</p>\n<p>Plan gradually, preserve access, and allow plants room to mature. A strong layered design is not the one with the greatest number of levels, but the one in which each height contributes to a coherent, healthy, and manageable garden.</p>\n"}]
